WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:10.690
Have you ever been in need of
a tool that you didn't have?

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:14.170
Imagine that you have this
screw that you need to unscrew,

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:17.050
but you don't have the right
screwdriver to do that.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.350
So what do you do?

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.150
Go to the nearest
hardware store.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.780
You look at the
various screwdrivers.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.150
They have bigger, smaller,
different screw heads.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.520
You pick the one that
will match your screw.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.720
You bring it home.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.860
You unscrew.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:34.850
You're good.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.870
Some time passes.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.820
You want to find
a table that will

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.150
match the space between
your sofa and the wall?

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.490
But you don't have a measuring
tape, and you need one.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:48.050
So what do you do?

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.970
We go to the nearest
hardware store.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.700
Look at the various
measuring tapes they have.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.620
Longer, shorter.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:55.860
Thinner, wider.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.660
Start wondering why
that even matters.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.700
But you need to make a decision.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.890
So we pick one.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:01.890
Bring it home.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.819
Measure. You're good.

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:06.500
Some time passes.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.630
You need to hammer a nail.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:12.050
So what do you do?

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.960
Start wondering,
wouldn't it make sense

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.300
to buy a complete
toolbox instead

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:21.300
of assembling one tool by tool?

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.740
My name is Kamil.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.650
I'm an full-stack
engineer at Appsilon.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.340
We specialize in building
Shiny applications.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.620
Let me tell you of
a typical situation

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.100
that we faced many
times over in our work.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.430
So a client comes to
us and asks whether we

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.950
could build an
application for them.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.770
Using wonderful
capabilities of Shiny,

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.450
we create something
within a couple

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:49.470
of hours that is interactive
and impresses the client.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:50.760
Contract signed.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.580
The project starts.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.970
Couple of weeks pass.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:57.000
The codebase grows
20 times and we realize

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.350
that we are spending a lot
of time discussing the code

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.590
style, where the opening
brackets should be.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.140
We figure that we need some
tools that will automatically

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.070
check this for us.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.570
We need a linter.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.090
So what do we do?

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.820
We find lintr, we
configure it properly,

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.450
we add it to the project.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:17.800
We start linting our code.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.600
We are good.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.880
Some time passes.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.330
We realize that we touch
one place in the application

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:28.000
and something else breaks.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.010
So we figured that we
should start writing tests.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:32.340
We need unit tests.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.850
What do we do?

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:34.910
We find testthat.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:35.860
We configure it.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.190
We add it to the project.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:38.950
We start writing unit tests.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.850
We're good.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:46.250
Some time passes and you get
the point. Needs keep arising

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:48.440
and we need to keep
addressing them.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.200
We start wondering,
wouldn't it make sense

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.920
to start with a complete
development toolbox

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.870
instead of assembling
one tool by tool?

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.840
This goes deeper, though, in
case of development tools.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.970
Introducing them later
on in the project

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.040
is typically more costly.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.350
For example, if you want to
add lintr when you already

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:13.490
have hundreds of lintr errors,
you first have to fix them all.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:15.560
And that's not
only a lot of work,

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.920
but you need to synchronize
it with your team.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.540
But we should take even a
wider perspective, though.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.190
It's not just one project.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:26.600
It's multiple teams dealing
with multiple projects

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:27.810
at the same time.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:31.250
And similar needs arise
in each of those projects,

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:34.280
and each team keeps
configuring the same tools.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:38.540
Wouldn't it be wonderful to
have just one universal toolbox?

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.310
All this effort
could be directed

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.310
into really polishing it instead
of reinventing the wheel all

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:44.480
the time.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.210
Moreover, in an
organization when

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.400
you have just one
universal toolbox,

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.500
people get proficient using it.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:55.100
So when you start a new project
or move to a different one,

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.920
everybody is up to speed
right from the beginning.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.540
This can really bring
the productivity

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.460
to a whole new level.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:05.390
So you can see what kind
of problem we have here.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:09.050
And this is where my personal
story begins, right here

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:10.040
in the middle.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.760
So I joined Appsilon
two years ago in July.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.150
It was a hot month.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.880
My brother was coming.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.149
It was the last month of
our only office being open.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:21.829
Pandemic was raging.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:24.050
Lots of things were happening.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:28.400
So my first project was
called project patterns.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:29.930
What was that?

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:33.470
Well, technically speaking,
it was a Git repository

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.950
with snippets of code on
branches and the related Python

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:39.050
tool that could be
used to combine them

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:42.740
into project structure for
new and existing projects.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:44.270
Sounds complicated.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:45.320
It was.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.020
And it was my task
to improve it.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.030
So, first of all,
I try to understand

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:51.590
the purpose of all this.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.470
And I started
researching what we try

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:56.090
to do about this in the past.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:00.300
And it turned out that we
did a lot. Like years before,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.630
already we had
discussion groups and we

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:04.340
had a repository
of code snippets

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.710
that could be reused
to some extent.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.660
Directly before
project patterns,

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:11.240
we had something
called we had set up

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:14.030
involving RStudio in
Docker and the related bash

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:17.210
tool that could be used to
automate some of the things.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:21.080
So I realized that already
many people before me

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.510
tried to solve these
problems that we had,

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:25.490
and I had the week
to improve that.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.920
You can guess that I didn't
manage to fix everything

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:31.350
within that week, but for
the following two years,

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.740
we kept improving what we had.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:36.800
For example, we went through
a phase of simplification

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:40.370
where instead of having all
this complex set of tools,

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:44.450
we created just a simple Shiny
template, a Git repository that

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:47.780
could be cloned and used as
a basis for your project.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.510
We also researched other tools.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:51.530
For example, we took
a look at gollem.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:54.180
We tried to learn how it
differs from our approach.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:55.440
We try to learn from it.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.460
We've considered whether
we could just use it.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.610
So for two years, we
kept addressing the needs

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:05.630
as they were arising, resolving
issues as they were surfacing.

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:08.630
And finally, in year
2022, we started

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.280
feeling that we have
something that we

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.110
want to share with the world.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:15.770
And thus, Rhino was born.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:17.420
No, not this rhino.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:20.480
Rhino the free and
open source R package,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:24.350
which we announced in May this
year during Appsilon Shiny

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:25.730
conference.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:30.050
So it's an R package, means
it has functions, for example,

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:32.060
this rhino init function.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.670
It creates a new
rhino project for you.

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:38.120
So what do we get inside?

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:42.080
Perhaps the most obvious part
is the full project structure

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:43.850
of files and directories.

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:47.060
So we have places for
configuration, source files,

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.260
dependencies, tests, everything
is covered from the beginning.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:55.670
So let's take a deeper
look at some of those.

00:06:55.670 --> 00:07:00.000
Any project will need other
R packages to do its job.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.170
And it's very important
to be explicit about what

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:06.860
these other packages are
and what versions you need.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.190
If you are not
explicit about that,

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:10.940
you might find that
when you try to launch

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.160
a project after a couple
of months, it doesn't work.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.470
Why?

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:16.140
Who knows?

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.340
Maybe some package
got updated and it broke.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:22.460
If you have an explicit
list of dependencies,

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:25.670
the chances that it will
just work are much higher.

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:27.590
And we have renv
package to deal

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:30.240
with this. For a similar issue,

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:32.240
but related to R
sources themselves,

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:33.770
we have the box package.

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:37.970
This one allows you to
use Python style imports

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:39.540
at the top of each file.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:42.080
So that for each file
it is immediately

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:47.810
obvious what packages, functions
and other files it needs.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:52.730
So moving on to the
quality of our code,

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:54.320
we have three
packages which deal

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.910
with that. For writing tests,

00:07:56.910 --> 00:08:00.320
We have testthat package.
Then we have lintr

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.300
which can be used to
automatically test

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:05.750
the style of your code and also
detect some errors that you

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:06.770
might have.

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:08.330
And we have the
styler package which

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:11.720
is able to fix many of those
problems automatically.

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:15.810
All these tools can be run using
Rhino functions out of the box.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.400
You don't need to
configure anything.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.720
Just do, for example,
rhino lint R,

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.380
and you get your code linted.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:26.900
So in enterprise
settings, typically you

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.720
will have some needs that
will be quite common.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:32.210
For example, one
of them is logging.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:35.059
It is very important to
output good log messages

00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:36.539
from your application.

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:38.720
If it breaks, for
example, in production,

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:40.490
this kind of log
messages can really

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:42.799
help you to find
what the issue is.

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:45.980
And we have the logger
package which deals with that.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:50.090
Then you typically will have
more than one environment.

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:51.270
You will have the local one

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.180
when you develop
the application,

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:56.180
Then, you will have staging
where you will be testing it

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:59.210
before pushing it to
production. And to manage

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:02.000
the differences between
these different environments,

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.020
we have the config package.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:09.820
Now in any big project, we
realize that sooner or later we

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:11.300
need JavaScript.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.990
We need to customize the
application beyond what's

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:15.700
provided by R packages.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:19.750
And we figured, why not use the
state of the art tech for that?

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:23.110
So for JavaScript, we will
run your code through Babel

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:25.540
and webpack this
to ensure that you

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:27.730
can use the latest
features without worrying

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:29.410
about browser support.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:31.930
We have ESLint that
you can use to check

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:34.060
the quality of your
JavaScript code

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:37.390
and also fix many of the
errors automatically.

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:40.930
While these things need Node.js
to be installed on your machine

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:42.550
when using it from
Rhino, you

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:45.130
will just call the
package functions build js,

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:49.970
lint js. Now similarly,
we typically

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:52.020
need to add some custom styling.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:53.780
And we chose Sass to do that.

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:55.880
It's an extension
for the CSS language

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:58.820
which allows you to
structure your code in a more

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:00.830
maintainable and better way.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:04.010
And again, here to
compile your Sass

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:06.260
into something understandable
by the browser,

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:10.160
we use the state of the art
latest implementation of Sass.

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:13.310
We also have style lint which
is able to lend your style

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:15.890
files automatically.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:19.450
Now, what else do you
need in a big project?

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.570
Well, certainly more testing.

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.230
And as we already
are using node.js,

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:27.410
we are using also
Cypress for end to end

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:29.570
testing of your application.

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:32.510
This framework allows you to
launch your whole application,

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:35.840
interact with it like
press buttons, type text,

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:38.720
and then verify if it
behaved as expected.

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:42.290
For example, that some text
appeared or window got opened.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:44.750
So again, this
can be used simply

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:49.070
by running a function of the
package called test and to end.

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:53.930
And finally, as a cherry
on top automation,

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:56.840
all these tools are useless
if you are not actually

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:57.860
running them.

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:00.110
And if it's not
happening automatically,

00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:03.050
sooner or later, you will
probably forget about it.

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:07.040
So if you use GitHub,
Rhino comes

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:11.250
with a working out of the box
set up for GitHub Actions.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:13.820
This will run all
the linters, unit

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:17.390
tests, end to end tests for
you automatically once you

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:20.200
initialize your project.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:20.700
All right.

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:24.560
So to summarize, what is Rhino?

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:28.610
Well, certainly you can think of
it as this complete development

00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:30.560
toolbox with all
those things that

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:33.110
have just listed and mentioned.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:35.060
But it's more than that.

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:37.700
We call it a framework.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:42.990
And if you know other frameworks,
go for Python or Ruby on rails.

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.350
We took a slightly
different angle here.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:48.410
While you do get the
project structure packages

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:52.340
to use and basic structure
for your application,

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:54.200
we also have all
these tools around it

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:56.600
for ensuring the
quality of your code.

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:58.880
So we can think of
Rhino as a framework,

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:02.150
but for the whole
development process.

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:06.980
Then Rhino is certainly the
Appsilon way to do things.

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:10.820
It is an opinionated framework
based on years of experience

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:13.490
that we have in building
Shiny applications.

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:16.220
It is not the only
way to do things,

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:18.290
but it is the way
that we believe in

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:20.450
and that we recommend.

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:24.020
And finally, Rhino is
great for enterprise.

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.080
While you can certainly use it
in your next proof of concept

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:30.080
project that you
will show to nobody.

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:33.170
Its benefits really start
to shine in organizations

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:35.960
when you have this one
universal toolbox, which

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:37.340
everybody can use.

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:42.020
It really can bring your
productivity to a new level.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:46.700
So I hope this got you a
bit interested in Rhino.

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:49.750
Perhaps maybe even
somewhat excited.

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:52.660
So if that's the case,
all you need to do

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:54.520
is to go to our website.

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:57.400
It contains an extensive
documentation for the package,

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:00.970
as we believe it is an essential
part of a framework like this.

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:04.330
So thank you for
listening and I encourage

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:07.200
you to use Rhino
in your next project.

